# gallery/.env: read by the gallery dev server (vite) and the operator
# scripts (node --env-file=.env scripts/...). Development only: an install
# takes its settings from the repository-root .env through compose.yaml
# (see ../.env.example). Copy to .env (git-ignored).
#
# The web images folder: <work>/<still>/wNNN.avif under it. In the
# container it is mounted read-only at /images; put a fast disk under it.
HIKARI_IMAGES=/path/to/gallery/images
# PostgreSQL (ADR-0010); the same credentials .env.db gives compose.db.yaml
DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:5432/NAME
# Admin sign-in (one account). scripts/admin-password.js writes all three.
# The hash uses colons, never "$": the dev server expands "$" in .env values.
HIKARI_ADMIN_USER=admin
HIKARI_ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=scrypt:SALTHEX:HASHHEX
HIKARI_SESSION_SECRET=32-random-bytes-hex
# Text encoder for mood search (the text-encoder service in compose.yaml,
# with its port published, or the image run by hand).
# Leave unset and mood search is simply unavailable; tag search keeps working.
ENCODER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5187
# Shoko, for the admin Onboard picker (read-only; a non-admin service key).
# Without them the Onboard page says Shoko is not configured. For
# development a read-only key against your own Shoko is fine; the client
# only ever sends the GET requests on its allowlist.
# HIKARI_SHOKO_BASE_URL=http://<shoko host>:<port>
# HIKARI_SHOKO_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/shoko-api-key
# Read-only mode: the gallery as a viewer only (no admin area, no worker
# API, nothing written to the database). For a copy strangers can reach
# (docs/HOW_IT_WORKS.md). Give it a database login that may only read.
# HIKARI_READ_ONLY=1
# The "Admin" link in the header of every page. ON unless this is set to
# 0 (or false, no, off): a gallery on your own network is your workbench,
# and the way in belongs in sight. Turn it off for an install the public
# can reach, so no page points at the sign-in. It is only the link: the
# sign-in itself does not depend on it, and /admin typed into the address
# bar still asks for the password either way. Never shown in read-only
# mode, which has no admin area at all.
# HIKARI_ADMIN_LINK=1
